   "family": "bredh",
   "is_root": true,
   "etymology": "* Singularized plural of *bradh, from Proto-Albanian *bardza, from Proto-Indo-European *bʰrh₁ǵós (“birch”). On the other hand, Matzinger reconstructs Proto-Albanian *braȷ́ʉh sg, *braȷ́ai pl. Compare Lithuanian béržas, Czech bříza, Ossetian бӕрз (bærz), English birch, Sanskrit भूर्ज (bhūrjá, “Himalayan birch”)). Possibly the source of Romanian brad (“fir”).\n* Alternatively, a part of a series of loan words from unattested ancient Greek dialects labeled as Macedonian because it was akin to the native idiom of the Greek-speaking population in the Argead Kingdom. Borrowed as Ancient Macedonian [script needed] (*brathy, “savin”), with Romanian brad having the same Greek dialectal origin, the Greek itself being of ultimate Semitic provenance).",
   "etymology_class": "inherited",
   "source_lang": null,
   "parents": [
    "birch"
   ],
